@node intro "Introduction"
@{b}Introduction@{ub}

AmigaGPT is a versatile multi-provider AI client for AmigaOS 3.x, AmigaOS 4.1 and MorphOS. It brings the capabilities of the leading AI providers - OpenAI, Google Gemini, xAI Grok, Anthropic Claude and any OpenAI-compatible server you host yourself - to your Amiga, enabling text generation, question answering and creative exploration. AmigaGPT can also generate images and speak its answers aloud, making it easier than ever to interact with AI on your Amiga. Designed to integrate seamlessly with your system, AmigaGPT delivers modern AI technology while embracing the timeless Amiga experience.

@{b}Features@{ub}

- @{b}State-of-the-art language models@{ub}
Chat with OpenAI (GPT-5, GPT-4, o1, o3, o4 and more), Google Gemini, xAI Grok, Anthropic Claude, or a custom OpenAI-compatible server such as LM Studio or Ollama. See @{"Chat Provider Settings" link chatprofiles}.

- @{b}AI image generation@{ub}
Create images with OpenAI (GPT-Image-1, DALL-E 3, DALL-E 2), Google Gemini or xAI Grok, view them in the app at any scale and save copies wherever you like. See @{"Generating images" link images}.

- @{b}File attachments@{ub}
Send files along with your prompt, and save files the model sends back. See @{"Attaching files" link attachments} and @{"Saving received files" link receivedfiles}.

- @{b}Shell Tool@{ub}
Let the AI run AmigaDOS commands on your system. Every command needs your approval before it runs. See @{"Shell Tool" link shelltool}.

- @{b}Code Interpreter@{ub}
Let the AI write and run Python in a provider-hosted sandbox for calculations, data analysis and generated files. See @{"Code Interpreter" link codeinterpreter}.

- @{b}Web search@{ub}
Let the AI look up current information on the web before it answers. See @{"Web search" link websearch}.

- @{b}Speech@{ub}
Seven speech systems are supported: narrator.device v34 and v37 on AmigaOS 3, flite.device on AmigaOS 4, and the cloud voices of OpenAI, xAI, ElevenLabs and OpenVox on every system. See @{"Speech" link speech}.

- @{b}Seamless integration with AmigaOS and MorphOS@{ub}
AmigaGPT takes full advantage of the MUI framework to provide a smooth, native user experience that is responsive and easy to use.

- @{b}UI customisation@{ub}
Choose the fonts, the colours, the text alignment and whether the app opens on the Workbench screen or on a screen of its own.

- @{b}ARexx integration@{ub}
Control AmigaGPT from scripts and from other applications. Send prompts, generate images and speak text with simple ARexx commands, with or without the GUI running. See @{"ARexx Integration" link arexx}.

- @{b}Full conversation history@{ub}
Every conversation and every generated image is saved and restored the next time you start the app.

- @{b}Localised@{ub}
AmigaGPT is translated into 22 languages in addition to English. See @{"Language and Catalogs" link language}.
@endnode

@node requirements "System Requirements"
@{b}System Requirements@{ub}

@{b}Hardware and operating system@{ub}

- An OCS/ECS/AGA Amiga, or a PowerPC machine capable of running AmigaOS 4.1 or MorphOS
- AmigaOS 3.1 or higher, AmigaOS 4.1, or MorphOS
- Motorola 68020 or higher CPU, or PowerPC for AmigaOS 4 and MorphOS
- 8MB Fast RAM minimum, 16MB or more recommended
- Internet access using a TCP/IP stack such as Roadshow (http://roadshow.apc-tcp.de/index-en.php)

@{b}Required software@{ub}

- MUI 3 minimum, MUI 5 recommended for all features
  MUI 5: https://github.com/amiga-mui/muidev/releases
  MUI 3.9: https://github.com/amiga-mui/muidev/releases/download/MUI-3.9-2015R1/MUI-3.9-2015R1-os3.lha
- render.library 32 or higher (https://aminet.net/package/dev/misc/renderlib68k)
- guigfx.library 20.0 or higher (https://aminet.net/dev/misc/guigfxlib.lha) or, for machines without an FPU, (https://aminet.net/dev/misc/guigfxlib_nofpu.lha)
- codesets.library 6.22 or higher (http://aminet.net/package/util/libs/codesets-6.22)
- MCC_Guigfx, the MUI custom class used to display images (http://aminet.net/package/dev/mui/MCC_Guigfx)
- MCC_NList, the MUI custom class used for lists (http://aminet.net/package/dev/mui/MCC_NList-0.128)
- MCC_TextEditor 15.56 or higher, the MUI custom class used for text input (https://aminet.net/dev/mui/MCC_TextEditor-15.56.lha)
- A PNG datatype for viewing generated images. Any will do, but here is one (https://aminet.net/util/dtype/vPNGdt.lha)
- For AmigaOS 3 and AmigaOS 4, AmiSSL 5.27 or higher
  OS3: https://aminet.net/package/util/libs/AmiSSL-v5-OS3
  OS4: https://aminet.net/package/util/libs/AmiSSL-v5-OS4
  MorphOS uses the system SSL support and does not need AmiSSL.

@{b}Accounts and keys@{ub}

- An API key for at least one of the supported providers - OpenAI, Google Gemini, xAI or Anthropic - or an OpenAI-compatible server of your own that needs no key at all. See @{"Entering your API key" link apikey}.

@{b}Optional@{ub}

- About 2.5MB of free space for magic.mgc, the file type database used to identify attachments. It is installed for you unless you say otherwise. See @{"How the file type is worked out" link filetypes}.
- AmigaOS 3 only, for offline speech: narrator.device, copied from your Workbench disks. See @{"Installing narrator.device v34" link narrator34} and @{"Installing narrator.device v37" link narrator37}.
- AmigaOS 3 only, for offline speech: translator.library v42 or higher, needed by both versions of narrator.device. Only v42 is required; the v43 patch is optional. See @{"Installing translator.library" link translator}.
- AmigaOS 4 only, for offline speech: Flite Device (http://aminet.net/package/mus/misc/flite_device)
- For the OpenAI, xAI, ElevenLabs and OpenVox voices: AHI (http://aminet.net/package/driver/audio/ahiusr_4.18)
- For ElevenLabs speech: an ElevenLabs account and API key
- For the GUI version of the AskGPT script: RxMUI and rmh.library
- On MorphOS, for ARexx support: the 68k version of ARexx
@endnode

@node installation "Installation"
@{b}Installation@{ub}

@{b}Before you start@{ub}

- On AmigaOS 3 and AmigaOS 4, install AmiSSL and a TCP/IP stack if you have not already done so
- Download and install MUI. Version 5 is recommended, version 3 is the minimum. Reboot afterwards.
- Download and install codesets.library, render.library, guigfx.library, MCC_Guigfx, MCC_NList and MCC_TextEditor

@{b}Installing AmigaGPT@{ub}

- Download the latest release of AmigaGPT
- Extract the AmigaGPT.lha archive to a temporary location such as RAM:
- Double click the Install-AmigaGPT icon and follow the prompts

@{"What the installer asks you" link installeroptions}

@{b}Optional: speech@{ub}

AmigaGPT can read its answers aloud. Cloud voices (OpenAI, xAI, ElevenLabs, OpenVox) work on every supported system and need only AHI. Offline voices are provided by the operating system and need extra files.

  @{"Installing AHI                       " link ahi}
  @{"Installing translator.library         " link translator}
  @{"Installing narrator.device v34       " link narrator34}
  @{"Installing narrator.device v37       " link narrator37}
  @{"Installing flite.device              " link flite}

On MorphOS there is no offline speech system; use one of the cloud voices with AHI.
@endnode

@node installeroptions "What the installer asks you"
@{b}What the installer asks you@{ub}

The installer is a standard Commodore Installer script and can be run in Novice, Average or Expert mode. It asks the following.

- @{b}Which version to install@{ub}
AmigaOS 3, AmigaOS 4 or MorphOS. The correct one is preselected for the machine you are running on.

- @{b}Where to install AmigaGPT@{ub}
A drawer named AmigaGPT is created inside the drawer you choose. Work: is a typical choice.

- @{b}Which languages to install@{ub}
English is built into the program and is always available. Any of the 22 translations can be installed as catalogs. Your system language is preselected. See @{"Language and Catalogs" link language}.

- @{b}Where to install the catalogs@{ub}
This should be the LOCALE: drawer at the root of your system.

- @{b}Where to install magic.mgc@{ub}
The file type database used to identify attachments. The AmigaGPT drawer is offered by default and is the recommended choice. Choose C: instead if you have, or plan to install, the "file" package from Aminet, so that both programs share one copy. AmigaGPT only looks in PROGDIR:, AMIGAGPT: and C:, so any other drawer will not be found. See @{"How the file type is worked out" link filetypes}.

- @{b}Where to install the guide@{ub}
Keep AmigaGPT.guide in the AmigaGPT drawer if you want the Help menu to be able to open it. AmigaGPT opens the guide as AMIGAGPT:AmigaGPT.guide.

- @{b}Where to install the readme@{ub}
Anywhere you like.

- @{b}The AMIGAGPT: assign@{ub}
An assign is added to S:User-Startup. AmigaGPT stores its configuration, conversation history and generated images through this assign, so it is required.

- @{b}Whether to start the daemon at boot@{ub}
Adds a line to S:User-Startup that runs AmigaGPTD when the machine boots, so ARexx scripts and the AskGPT command work without the GUI being open. See @{"The AmigaGPTD daemon" link daemon}.

- @{b}Whether to install the AskGPT script@{ub}
Installs the AskGPT ARexx script into C: so you can ask questions from the Shell. See @{"The AskGPT shell command" link askgpt}.

You must reboot, or execute the new S:User-Startup lines by hand, before AmigaGPT will run.
@endnode

@node ahi "Installing AHI"
@{b}Installing AHI@{ub}

AHI is the Amiga audio system. AmigaGPT needs it to play the 16 bit audio returned by the OpenAI, xAI, ElevenLabs and OpenVox voices. It is not needed for narrator.device or flite.device.

Many systems already have AHI. If yours does not, download it from http://aminet.net/package/driver/audio/ahiusr_4.18 and run the installer in the archive.

After installing, use the AHI preferences program to select a working audio mode for your hardware.
@endnode

@node translator "Installing translator.library"
@{b}Installing translator.library@{ub}

translator.library turns English text into the phonemes that narrator.device speaks. Both v34 and v37 of narrator.device need it.

@{b}Version 42 is all AmigaGPT requires@{ub}, and it is available as a standalone install:

- Download http://aminet.net/util/libs/translator42.lha and extract the archive somewhere convenient on your Amiga, such as RAM:
- Enter that drawer and double click the Install program
- Run the installer using all the default settings
- Reboot your Amiga. It will not work until the system is restarted.

@{b}Optional: patching to v43@{ub}

Version 43 is a later revision with improved pronunciation. AmigaGPT does not need it - earlier versions of this guide said it was required, but that is no longer the case - and speech works perfectly well on v42. If you would like it anyway, install v42 as above and then apply the patch:

- Download http://aminet.net/util/libs/Tran43pch.lha and extract it to a location of your choice
- Enter that drawer and double click the Install program
- Run the installer using all the default settings
- Reboot your Amiga
@endnode

@node narrator34 "Installing narrator.device v34"
@{b}Installing narrator.device v34@{ub}

v34 is the original speech synthesiser that came with Workbench 1.x. It cannot be included with AmigaGPT because it is still under copyright, so you must copy it from your own Workbench disks.

Insert your Workbench 1.x disk and copy df0:devs/narrator.device to AMIGAGPT:devs/speech/34

You also need @{"translator.library v42 or higher" link translator}.

Once it is in place, choose the "Workbench 1.x v34" speech system in @{"Speech Provider Settings" link speechprofiles}.
@endnode

@node narrator37 "Installing narrator.device v37"
@{b}Installing narrator.device v37@{ub}

v37 is the updated synthesiser included with Workbench 2.0.x. It has more features and sounds more natural than v34, but it also sounds quite different, which is why AmigaGPT lets you install both and switch between them.

Insert your Workbench 2.0.x disk and copy df0:devs/narrator.device to AMIGAGPT:devs/speech/37

You cannot use a Workbench 2.1 disk, because the speech libraries were removed after version 2.0.4.

You also need @{"translator.library v42 or higher" link translator}.

Once it is in place, choose the "Workbench 2.0 v37" speech system in @{"Speech Provider Settings" link speechprofiles}.
@endnode

@node flite "Installing flite.device"
@{b}Installing flite.device@{ub}

Offline speech on AmigaOS 4.1 is provided by Flite Device, a port of the Festival Lite speech synthesiser.

- Download Flite Device from http://aminet.net/package/mus/misc/flite_device
- Extract the archive and run the installer

Once it is installed, choose the "Flite" speech system in @{"Speech Provider Settings" link speechprofiles} and pick one of the five Flite voices: kal, kal16, awb, rms or slt.
@endnode

@node launching "Launching AmigaGPT"
@{b}Launching AmigaGPT@{ub}

@{b}From Workbench@{ub}

Double click the AmigaGPT icon for the operating system you are running. The icon carries the correct stack size, so nothing else is needed.

@{b}From the Shell@{ub}

AmigaGPT needs 32KB of stack. The default stack for a program started from the Shell is 4KB, which is not enough and will cause random crashes through stack overflow. Set the stack first:

  STACK 32768
  AMIGAGPT:AmigaGPT

On AmigaOS 3, AmigaGPT checks its own stack on startup and warns you if it is below 32768 bytes.

@{b}The daemon@{ub}

AmigaGPTD is a companion program with no user interface that answers the same ARexx commands as the full application. Run it from the Shell, or let the installer add it to S:User-Startup so that it is always available. See @{"The AmigaGPTD daemon" link daemon}.

@{b}First run@{ub}

Before you can chat you need to give AmigaGPT an API key for the provider you want to use. See @{"Entering your API key" link apikey}.
@endnode

