@node mainwindow "The main window"
@{b}The main window@{ub}

AmigaGPT has two modes of operation, selected by the tabs at the top left of the window:

- @{b}Chat Mode@{ub} - talk to a language model
- @{b}Image Generation Mode@{ub} - create pictures from a description

@{b}Chat Mode@{ub}

  - The @{b}conversation list@{ub} runs down the left hand side, with @{b}New Chat@{ub} and @{b}Delete Chat@{ub} beneath it
  - The @{b}transcript@{ub} fills the centre of the window and shows the whole conversation, your messages and the assistant replies
  - The @{b}input field@{ub} sits below the transcript
  - @{b}Send@{ub} submits the message. Shift + Enter in the input field does the same.
  - @{b}Stop speaking@{ub} interrupts speech that is currently playing
  - @{b}Attach files...@{ub} and @{b}Clear@{ub} manage the files sent with the next message
  - @{b}Save received files...@{ub} becomes available when a reply contains files

@{b}Image Generation Mode@{ub}

  - The @{b}image list@{ub} runs down the left hand side, with @{b}New Image@{ub} and @{b}Delete Image@{ub} beneath it
  - The generated picture is displayed in the centre of the window
  - The @{b}prompt field@{ub} sits below the picture
  - @{b}Create Image@{ub} generates the picture
  - @{b}Open Image@{ub} opens the picture at whatever scale you want
  - @{b}Save Image Copy@{ub} writes a copy somewhere else on your Amiga

@{b}The status bar@{ub}

A status bar along the bottom of the window shows what AmigaGPT is doing - "Ready", "Sending message...", "Executing command...", "Loading image...", "Generating conversation title..." and so on - together with a progress bar while a request is running.
@endnode

@node apikey "Entering your API key"
@{b}Entering your API key@{ub}

Before you can chat you must tell AmigaGPT which provider to use and give it a key for that provider.

- Open @{b}Chat Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Chat@{ub} menu
- Select the provider in the @{b}Profiles@{ub} list on the left
- Type or paste your key into the @{b}API key@{ub} field
- Press @{b}OK@{ub}

Where to get a key:

- @{b}OpenAI@{ub} - https://platform.openai.com/account/api-keys
- @{b}Google Gemini@{ub} - https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
- @{b}xAI Grok@{ub} - https://console.x.ai
- @{b}Anthropic Claude@{ub} - https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys

Keys are stored per profile and per subsystem, so the key you use for chat, the key you use for image generation and the key you use for speech can all be different. A locally hosted server such as LM Studio usually needs no key at all; leave the field empty and set @{b}Authorization@{ub} to @{b}None@{ub}.

The image and speech subsystems have their own settings windows: @{"Image Provider Settings" link imageprofiles} and @{"Speech Provider Settings" link speechprofiles}.

Your keys are written to AMIGAGPT:config.json in plain text. Anyone who can read that file can read your keys.
@endnode

@node chatting "Chatting"
@{b}Chatting@{ub}

Type a prompt into the input field at the bottom of the window and press @{b}Send@{ub}, or Shift + Enter. The reply appears in the transcript above, streamed a piece at a time where the provider supports it. If speech is enabled the reply is also read aloud.

Your messages and the replies are formatted differently so they are easy to tell apart, and you can change the alignment of each in the @{b}View@{ub} menu. See @{"Changing the appearance" link appearance}.

@{b}Choosing the model@{ub}

Open @{b}Chat Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Chat@{ub} menu to choose the provider and the model, and to turn @{b}Web Search@{ub}, @{b}Shell Tool@{ub}, @{b}Code Interpreter@{ub} and @{b}Streaming@{ub} on or off. See @{"Chat Provider Settings" link chatprofiles}.

@{b}The system prompt@{ub}

Each chat profile has its own @{b}Chat System@{ub} field. Whatever you put there is sent to the model as a standing instruction ahead of every message in that conversation - for example "Always answer in French" or "You are a terse Amiga assistant". Leave it empty if you do not want one.

@{b}Conversation titles@{ub}

AmigaGPT asks the model for a short title for each new conversation and uses it to name the entry in the conversation list.

@{b}Length limits@{ub}

There is no fixed limit on the length of a conversation. How long it can grow is decided by the memory in your Amiga and by the context window of the model you are using.

@{b}Unicode@{ub}

AmigaGPT instructs the server not to use characters the Amiga cannot display, such as emoji, because they would appear as question marks.

@{b}Chatting without the GUI@{ub}

You can also chat from the Shell with the AskGPT command, and from any program that can talk ARexx. See @{"The AskGPT shell command" link askgpt} and @{"ARexx Integration" link arexx}.
@endnode

@node attachments "Attaching files"
@{b}Attaching files@{ub}

Press @{b}Attach files...@{ub} to pick one or more files to send with your next message. The number of files waiting to be sent is shown beside the button, and @{b}Clear@{ub} removes them all again. The attachments are sent when you press @{b}Send@{ub}, and they are recorded in the transcript alongside your message.

@{b}How the files are sent@{ub}

OpenAI, xAI Grok, Anthropic Claude and Google Gemini upload the file first and then refer to it by its identifier, so the chat request itself stays small - which matters a great deal on a classic Amiga TCP stack. Other providers receive images and documents in their own multimodal request format.

@{b}Size limits@{ub}

To keep memory use practical on classic Amigas, each file may be up to 8MB, and all the files in one request may total up to 16MB.

@{b}ZIP archives@{ub}

A .zip file is sent as application/zip. On OpenAI it is placed in the Code Interpreter container, and on Anthropic Claude it is uploaded to the code-execution container. Turn on @{b}Code Interpreter@{ub} for that profile so the model can unpack it. See @{"Code Interpreter" link codeinterpreter}.

@{b}What a provider accepts@{ub}

Which file types a provider and model will accept varies. If a file is rejected, the error from the server is shown in a requester.

See also @{"How the file type is worked out" link filetypes}.
@endnode

@node receivedfiles "Saving received files"
@{b}Saving received files@{ub}

Some replies contain files rather than just text - a CSV produced by the @{"Code Interpreter" link codeinterpreter}, a chart, a document the model generated. When that happens the files are listed underneath the assistant message and the @{b}Save received files...@{ub} button becomes available.

Press it and choose a destination drawer. AmigaGPT downloads every file in the reply and writes it there. Nothing is ever overwritten: if a file of that name already exists, a number is added to the new one.

The status bar reports how many files were saved.
@endnode

@node filetypes "How the file type is worked out"
@{b}How the file type is worked out@{ub}

AmigaGPT has to tell the provider what kind of file each attachment is. It works this out by looking inside the file with libmagic, the same code the Unix "file" command uses, rather than by trusting the file name.

This matters on the Amiga, where files often have no extension at all. A text file called S:User-Startup is correctly sent as plain text, and a file with a misleading name is sent as what it really is rather than what it claims to be.

@{b}The magic.mgc database@{ub}

libmagic needs a database of file signatures called magic.mgc. AmigaGPT looks for it in three places, in this order:

  1. PROGDIR:magic.mgc - the drawer AmigaGPT itself is in
  2. AMIGAGPT:magic.mgc - the same drawer, reached through the assign
  3. C:magic.mgc - where the "file" package from Aminet puts it

The installer offers to put it in the AmigaGPT drawer, which is the recommended choice, or in C: so that one copy can be shared with the "file" package. No other location is searched.

The copy shipped with AmigaGPT is a reduced database. The full one is over 10MB, which is too large for a 68k machine to load comfortably.

@{b}If the database is missing@{ub}

Attachments still work. AmigaGPT falls back to guessing the type from the file name extension, exactly as earlier versions always did.
@endnode

@node conlist "The conversation list"
@{b}The conversation list@{ub}

The list down the left hand side of the window in Chat Mode holds every conversation you have had. Click an entry to go back to it; the whole transcript is restored, and anything you say next continues that conversation.

- @{b}New Chat@{ub} starts a fresh conversation
- @{b}Delete Chat@{ub} removes the selected one

Conversations are saved to AMIGAGPT:chat-history.json as you go, so they are still there the next time you start AmigaGPT.

The daemon keeps its own separate history. See @{"The AmigaGPTD daemon" link daemon}.
@endnode

@node images "Generating images"
@{b}Generating images@{ub}

Switch to @{b}Image Generation Mode@{ub} with the tab at the top left of the window.

- Open @{b}Image Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Image@{ub} menu to choose the provider, the model, the image size and the file format
- Type a description of the picture you want into the prompt field
- Press @{b}Create Image@{ub}

When the picture has been downloaded it is displayed in the window and added to the image list on the left.

- @{b}Open Image@{ub} opens it at whatever scale you like
- @{b}Save Image Copy@{ub} writes a copy anywhere on your Amiga
- @{b}New Image@{ub} clears the prompt ready for the next one
- @{b}Delete Image@{ub} removes the selected picture

AmigaGPT gives each picture a short name generated from the prompt, and keeps every picture it generates in AMIGAGPT:images until you delete it, so saving a copy elsewhere is only needed if you want one.

@{b}Providers, sizes and formats@{ub}

Image generation is supported by OpenAI, Google Gemini and xAI Grok, and by custom profiles that use an images/generations or Gemini generateContent endpoint. Anthropic Claude does not generate images.

The available sizes are 256x256, 512x512, 1024x1024, 1792x1024, 1024x1792, 1536x1024, 1024x1536 and auto. Which of them a model accepts depends on the model, and some providers ignore the size altogether. The format can be PNG or JPEG.

See @{"Image Provider Settings" link imageprofiles}.
@endnode

@node shelltool "Shell Tool"
@{b}Shell Tool@{ub}

The Shell Tool lets the AI run AmigaDOS commands on your Amiga. It can list files, create drawers, show the contents of a file, report your Workbench version, check free memory and so on, and use what it finds to answer you.

@{b}Enabling it@{ub}

Open @{b}Chat Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Chat@{ub} menu and turn on @{b}Shell Tool@{ub} for the profile you are using. The setting is remembered per profile.

@{b}Example prompts@{ub}

- "List all files in RAM:"
- "Create a new drawer called Test in Work:"
- "Show me the contents of S:Startup-Sequence"
- "What version of Workbench am I running?"
- "How much free memory do I have?"

@{b}Approval@{ub}

AmigaGPT never runs a command without asking. A requester shows you the exact command the AI wants to run and offers @{b}Allow@{ub} and @{b}Deny@{ub}. If you deny it, the model is told so and carries on without it. The command, its exit code and its output are all written into the transcript so you can see exactly what happened.

@{b}AmigaDOS syntax@{ub}

The AI is automatically told how AmigaDOS differs from Unix, so it uses the right commands:

  list or dir   instead of   ls
  type          instead of   cat
  makedir       instead of   mkdir
  delete        instead of   rm
  copy          instead of   cp
  rename        instead of   mv

It also understands Amiga path conventions such as Work:Projects/myfile.

@{b}ARexx@{ub}

The Shell Tool also works through the ARexx SENDMESSAGE command, in both AmigaGPT and the AmigaGPTD daemon.

@{b}Warning@{ub}

Use this feature responsibly. You control which commands are allowed to run, but be careful about approving commands that modify or delete files.
@endnode

@node codeinterpreter "Code Interpreter"
@{b}Code Interpreter@{ub}

The Code Interpreter lets the AI write and run Python in a sandbox hosted by the chat provider. Nothing runs on your Amiga. Use it for arithmetic the model would otherwise get wrong, for data analysis, and for producing files as part of an answer.

@{b}Enabling it@{ub}

Open @{b}Chat Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Chat@{ub} menu and turn on @{b}Code Interpreter@{ub} for the selected profile.

The option is offered for OpenAI, Google Gemini, xAI Grok and Anthropic Claude. For a custom profile it appears when the API endpoint is responses, messages or Gemini generateContent, and is hidden for chat/completions servers, which have no hosted interpreter.

@{b}Sending data in@{ub}

Attach a file with @{b}Attach files...@{ub}. A .zip archive is handed to the Code Interpreter container on OpenAI, and to the code-execution container on Anthropic Claude, so the model can unpack it and work through the contents. See @{"Attaching files" link attachments}.

@{b}Getting results out@{ub}

When the model creates a file - a CSV, a plot, a report - it is listed under the reply and can be written to disk with @{b}Save received files...@{ub}. See @{"Saving received files" link receivedfiles}.
@endnode

@node websearch "Web search"
@{b}Web search@{ub}

Web search lets the model look up current information before it answers, instead of relying only on what it learned during training. Ask about today's news, a current price or a recent release and it will search the web and cite what it found.

@{b}Enabling it@{ub}

Open @{b}Chat Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Chat@{ub} menu and turn on @{b}Web Search@{ub} for the profile you are using. The setting is remembered per profile.

@{b}From ARexx@{ub}

The @{b}WEBSEARCH@{ub} switch on the ARexx SENDMESSAGE command turns web search on for a single request, even if the profile has it switched off. It cannot turn it off for a profile that has it on. See @{"SENDMESSAGE" link arexxsendmessage}.

@{b}Availability@{ub}

Web search is a server-side feature. It works where the provider supports it; a custom server that does not offer it will simply ignore the request.
@endnode

@node printing "Printing a conversation"
@{b}Printing a conversation@{ub}

@{b}Print@{ub} in the @{b}Project@{ub} menu, or the keyboard shortcut Right-Amiga + P, sends the conversation currently on screen to your printer using the standard Amiga printer device. Set your printer up in Preferences first.
@endnode

@node editmenu "Editing text"
@{b}Editing text@{ub}

The @{b}Edit@{ub} menu holds the usual editing commands. They act on whichever text field has the keyboard focus, and they are greyed out when neither the chat input nor the image prompt is active.

  @{b}Cut@{ub}         Right-Amiga + X
  @{b}Copy@{ub}        Right-Amiga + C
  @{b}Paste@{ub}       Right-Amiga + V
  @{b}Clear@{ub}       Right-Amiga + L
  @{b}Select all@{ub}  Right-Amiga + A
@endnode

@node screen "Choosing a screen"
@{b}Choosing a screen@{ub}

AmigaGPT can run on the Workbench screen or on a screen of its own. This is handled by MUI rather than by AmigaGPT itself: open @{b}MUI Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}View@{ub} menu and use the @{b}Screen@{ub} or @{b}System@{ub} page.

If you choose a screen of its own you can define exactly what that screen looks like. AmigaGPT works at anything from 320x200 up to 4K resolution on an RTG graphics card.

Bear in mind that text becomes very small above 1080p, so you may want to raise the font sizes in MUI Settings once the app has opened.

@{b}Clear MUI Settings@{ub} in the @{b}View@{ub} menu throws away AmigaGPT's saved MUI configuration and returns everything - screen, fonts, colours, window positions - to the defaults.
@endnode

@node appearance "Changing the appearance"
@{b}Changing the appearance@{ub}

@{b}The View menu@{ub}

- @{b}Fixed width fonts@{ub} switches the transcript and the input field to a monospaced font, which makes code and tables in the replies line up properly
- @{b}User text alignment@{ub} sets your own messages to Left, Center or Right in the transcript
- @{b}Assistant text alignment@{ub} does the same for the replies
- @{b}MUI Settings...@{ub} opens the full MUI preferences for this application - fonts, colours, frames, screen and window behaviour
- @{b}Clear MUI Settings@{ub} resets all of that back to the defaults

@{b}Formatting in replies@{ub}

AmigaGPT renders bold, italic and underline from the model's markdown into the transcript's own soft styles, so replies read naturally instead of being littered with asterisks.

All of these settings are saved and restored automatically.
@endnode

@node proxy "Using a proxy server"
@{b}Using a proxy server@{ub}

The @{b}Connection@{ub} menu has a @{b}Proxy@{ub} submenu with two items: @{b}Enabled@{ub}, which switches the proxy on and off, and @{b}Settings...@{ub}, which opens the proxy configuration window.

The settings window asks for:

- @{b}Host@{ub} - the address of the proxy server
- @{b}Port@{ub} - its port number, from 0 to 65535
- @{b}Encryption@{ub} - @{b}None (fastest)@{ub} or @{b}SSL (secure)@{ub}
- @{b}Authentication@{ub} - @{b}No authentication@{ub} or @{b}Username and password@{ub}
- @{b}Username@{ub} and @{b}Password@{ub}, when authentication is required

@{b}Why you might want one@{ub}

Both HTTP and HTTPS proxies are supported. If you use an unencrypted HTTP proxy on your local network, the encryption work is done by the proxy machine instead of by your Amiga, which can make AmigaGPT noticeably faster on slower hardware. mitmproxy (https://mitmproxy.org) is an easy proxy to set up for this.

The proxy applies to all requests. ARexx callers can also override it per request. See @{"SENDMESSAGE" link arexxsendmessage}.
@endnode

@node providers "Providers, Profiles and Models"
@{b}Providers, Profiles and Models@{ub}

AmigaGPT is not tied to a single AI company. Chat, image generation and speech each have their own list of @{b}profiles@{ub}, and each profile holds everything needed to talk to one server: the host, the port, the encryption, the authorisation, the API key, the model and the options for that subsystem.

Every list starts with @{b}built-in@{ub} profiles that cannot be deleted, and you may add as many @{b}custom@{ub} profiles of your own as you like.

  @{"Chat Provider Settings          " link chatprofiles}
  @{"Image Provider Settings         " link imageprofiles}
  @{"Speech Provider Settings        " link speechprofiles}
  @{"Custom provider profiles        " link customprofiles}
  @{"Models                          " link models}
@endnode

@node chatprofiles "Chat Provider Settings"
@{b}Chat Provider Settings@{ub}

Open @{b}Chat Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Chat@{ub} menu.

@{b}The profile list@{ub}

The @{b}Profiles@{ub} list on the left holds the built-in providers first - @{b}OpenAI@{ub}, @{b}Google Gemini@{ub}, @{b}xAI Grok@{ub} and @{b}Anthropic Claude@{ub} - followed by any custom profiles. Whichever profile is selected when you press @{b}OK@{ub} becomes the one used for chatting.

Beneath the list are @{b}New@{ub}, which adds a blank profile called "New Profile", @{b}Duplicate@{ub}, which copies the selected profile and appends " copy" to its name, and @{b}Delete@{ub}, which removes a custom profile. Built-in providers cannot be deleted.

If you change something and then select a different profile without saving, AmigaGPT asks whether to keep your changes, offering @{b}Yes@{ub}, @{b}No@{ub} and @{b}Cancel@{ub}.

@{b}Settings@{ub}

- @{b}Profile Name@{ub} - the name shown in the list. A custom profile may not be given the name of a built-in provider, and two custom profiles may not share a name.
- @{b}Host@{ub} and @{b}Port@{ub} - the server to connect to. Disabled for built-in providers, whose addresses are fixed.
- @{b}Encryption@{ub} - @{b}None (fastest)@{ub} or @{b}SSL (secure)@{ub}. Disabled for built-in providers.
- @{b}Authorization@{ub} - how the key is presented to the server: @{b}None@{ub} or @{b}Bearer Token@{ub}. Disabled for built-in providers.
- @{b}API key@{ub} - your key for this provider. Always editable, including for built-in providers.
- @{b}Chat Model@{ub} - the model to use. A list of known models is offered for each built-in provider.
- @{b}Fetch Models@{ub} - asks the server which models it currently offers and refills the list from the answer. Available for built-in providers as well as custom ones.
- @{b}API Endpoint@{ub} - which API dialect to speak: @{b}responses@{ub}, @{b}chat/completions@{ub}, @{b}messages@{ub} or @{b}models/:generateContent@{ub}. Disabled for built-in providers.
- @{b}API Endpoint URL@{ub} - the path on the server. Disabled for built-in providers.
- @{b}Custom Headers@{ub} - extra HTTP headers to send with every request. Disabled for built-in providers.
- @{b}Full URL Preview@{ub} - shows the complete URL that the current settings will produce, so you can see at a glance whether the host, port and endpoint fit together.
- @{b}Chat System@{ub} - the system prompt for this profile. See @{"Chatting" link chatting}.
- @{b}Streaming@{ub} - @{b}On@{ub} shows the reply as it arrives, word by word. @{b}Off@{ub} waits for the whole reply. Turn it off if your server does not support streaming.
- @{b}Web Search@{ub} - see @{"Web search" link websearch}
- @{b}Shell Tool@{ub} - see @{"Shell Tool" link shelltool}
- @{b}Code Interpreter@{ub} - see @{"Code Interpreter" link codeinterpreter}. Hidden for profiles whose endpoint has no hosted interpreter.

@{b}Test@{ub}

@{b}Test@{ub} sends a small request to the server using the settings currently on screen and reports @{b}PASS@{ub} or @{b}FAIL@{ub}, with the raw response available so you can see exactly what the server said. It is the quickest way to find a wrong key, a wrong endpoint or a server that is not running.
@endnode

@node imageprofiles "Image Provider Settings"
@{b}Image Provider Settings@{ub}

Open @{b}Image Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Image@{ub} menu. The window works exactly like @{"Chat Provider Settings" link chatprofiles}, with the same profile list and the same New, Duplicate, Delete and Test buttons, but it carries the settings that matter for pictures.

The built-in image providers are @{b}OpenAI@{ub}, @{b}Google Gemini@{ub} and @{b}xAI Grok@{ub}. Anthropic Claude does not generate images and so does not appear here.

Settings that differ from the chat window:

- @{b}Image Size@{ub} - 256x256, 512x512, 1024x1024, 1792x1024, 1024x1792, 1536x1024, 1024x1536 or auto. Which sizes a model accepts depends on the model, and some providers ignore the setting entirely.
- @{b}Image Format@{ub} - @{b}png@{ub} or @{b}jpeg@{ub}
- @{b}API Endpoint@{ub} - @{b}images/generations@{ub} for the OpenAI-compatible image API, or @{b}models/:generateContent@{ub} for Gemini's native image endpoint

The chat options - streaming, web search, shell tool, code interpreter and the system prompt - are not shown, because they do not apply to image generation.

Image profiles keep their own API keys, separate from the chat profiles, so you can use different keys for chat and for images with the same provider.
@endnode

@node customprofiles "Custom provider profiles"
@{b}Custom provider profiles@{ub}

A custom profile lets you point AmigaGPT at any server that speaks one of the supported API dialects - LM Studio or Ollama on a machine on your own network, a company gateway, a proxy in front of a provider, or a provider AmigaGPT does not know about.

Press @{b}New@{ub} in @{"Chat Provider Settings" link chatprofiles}, @{"Image Provider Settings" link imageprofiles} or @{"Speech Provider Settings" link speechprofiles} and fill in the fields. Everything is editable for a custom profile, unlike a built-in one.

@{b}What to fill in@{ub}

- @{b}Host@{ub} - the machine name or IP address, with no protocol prefix. For a server on your own network this is something like 192.168.1.10.
- @{b}Port@{ub} - 443 is usual for SSL, 80 or 1234 for a local server
- @{b}Encryption@{ub} - use @{b}None (fastest)@{ub} for a server on your own network. Plain HTTP saves a great deal of work on a 68k Amiga.
- @{b}Authorization@{ub} - @{b}None@{ub} if the server needs no key, otherwise @{b}Bearer Token@{ub}
- @{b}API Endpoint@{ub} - the dialect the server speaks. Most local servers imitate OpenAI and want @{b}chat/completions@{ub}.
- @{b}API Endpoint URL@{ub} - the path, for example v1/chat/completions. Check the @{b}Full URL Preview@{ub} to make sure the result is what your server expects.
- @{b}Custom Headers@{ub} - anything else the server requires

Then press @{b}Fetch Models@{ub} to pull the model list from the server, choose one, and press @{b}Test@{ub} to confirm the whole thing works before you press @{b}OK@{ub}.

@{b}Limitations@{ub}

Local servers generally have no Files API, so attachments are sent inline in the request rather than uploaded first, and there is usually no hosted code interpreter, so the @{b}Code Interpreter@{ub} option is hidden for chat/completions profiles.
@endnode

@node models "Models"
@{b}Models@{ub}

AmigaGPT ships with a list of known models for each built-in provider, and @{b}Fetch Models@{ub} asks the server for its current list at any time. Because new models appear constantly, the fetched list is always more up to date than the built-in one.

@{b}Where to read about the models@{ub}

- OpenAI - https://platform.openai.com/docs/models
- Google Gemini - https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models
- xAI Grok - https://docs.x.ai/docs/models
- Anthropic Claude - https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/about-claude/models

@{b}Choosing one@{ub}

On a classic Amiga the size of the reply matters as much as the quality of the model, because every character has to come down the wire and be rendered. The smaller and faster models - for example gpt-5-mini, gemini-2.5-flash, grok-4-fast or claude-3-5-haiku - are usually the more comfortable choice, and the large reasoning models are worth the wait only when the question really needs them.

@{b}From ARexx@{ub}

@{b}LISTMODELS@{ub} asks a profile's server what it offers, and @{b}MODEL@{ub} on SENDMESSAGE and CREATEIMAGE overrides the model for a single request. See @{"ARexx Integration" link arexx}.
@endnode

@node speech "Speech"
@{b}Speech@{ub}

AmigaGPT can read the assistant's replies aloud, and can speak arbitrary text on demand from ARexx.

@{b}Turning it on@{ub}

Tick @{b}Enabled@{ub} in the @{b}Speech@{ub} menu. Speech runs in the background, so the application stays responsive while it is talking, and the @{b}Stop speaking@{ub} button interrupts it at any point.

If speech is switched off, AmigaGPT does not open the speech device at all, so a machine without AHI installed sees no error at startup.

@{b}The seven speech systems@{ub}

  @{b}Workbench 1.x v34@{ub}  AmigaOS 3.  Offline.  narrator.device v34 + translator
  @{b}Workbench 2.0 v37@{ub}  AmigaOS 3.  Offline.  narrator.device v37 + translator
  @{b}Flite@{ub}              AmigaOS 4.  Offline.  flite.device
  @{b}OpenAI@{ub}             Any system. Cloud.    AHI. 16 bit voices.
  @{b}xAI@{ub}                Any system. Cloud.    AHI.
  @{b}ElevenLabs@{ub}         Any system. Cloud.    AHI and an ElevenLabs key.
  @{b}OpenVox@{ub}            Any system. Your own server. AHI.

MorphOS has no offline speech system; use one of the cloud voices.

@{b}Choosing and configuring one@{ub}

Everything to do with speech now lives in one window: @{b}Speech Provider Settings...@{ub} in the @{b}Speech@{ub} menu. See @{"Speech Provider Settings" link speechprofiles}.

@{b}Speaking from ARexx@{ub}

The @{b}SPEAKTEXT@{ub} command speaks any text you give it, and can write the audio to a file instead of playing it. See @{"SPEAKTEXT" link arexxspeaktext}.
@endnode

@node speechprofiles "Speech Provider Settings"
@{b}Speech Provider Settings@{ub}

Open @{b}Speech Provider Settings...@{ub} from the @{b}Speech@{ub} menu. This is where the speech system is chosen and configured. In earlier versions of AmigaGPT this was done from the Speech menu itself.

@{b}The profile list@{ub}

As with the chat and image windows, the @{b}Profiles@{ub} list holds the built-in speech systems for your operating system followed by any profiles of your own, and has @{b}New@{ub}, @{b}Duplicate@{ub} and @{b}Delete@{ub} beneath it. The profile selected when you press @{b}OK@{ub} is the one that will be used. Because each profile stores a complete set of settings, you can keep several voices ready and switch between them in a couple of clicks.

@{b}Common settings@{ub}

- @{b}Profile Name@{ub}
- @{b}Speech system@{ub} - which of the seven systems this profile uses
- @{b}Test@{ub} - speaks a sample using the settings currently on screen

@{b}Connection settings@{ub}

Shown for the cloud systems: @{b}Host@{ub}, @{b}Port@{ub}, @{b}Encryption@{ub}, @{b}Authorization@{ub} and @{b}API Endpoint URL@{ub}. For a built-in provider these are fixed. An OpenVox profile defaults to host 127.0.0.1 and port 8666, which is a server running on the same machine or elsewhere on your own network.

@{b}Workbench narrator.device@{ub}

Shown for the v34 and v37 systems on AmigaOS 3:

- @{b}Accent@{ub} - the accent file translator.library should load. The default is american.accent. Other accents can be added; there is a well known tutorial on adding a French one.
- @{b}Rate (wpm)@{ub} - speaking speed in words per minute. The default is 150.
- @{b}Pitch (Hz)@{ub} - the pitch of the voice. The default is 110.
- @{b}Mode@{ub} - @{b}Natural@{ub} or @{b}Robotic@{ub}
- @{b}Sex@{ub} - @{b}Male@{ub} or @{b}Female@{ub}

Each of v34 and v37 keeps its own copy of these settings.

@{b}Flite@{ub}

- @{b}Flite Voice@{ub} - kal, kal16, awb, rms or slt

@{b}OpenAI@{ub}

- @{b}API key@{ub}
- @{b}Model@{ub} - tts-1, tts-1-hd or gpt-4o-mini-tts, with @{b}Fetch Models@{ub} to refresh the list from the server
- @{b}Voice@{ub} - alloy, ash, ballad, coral, echo, fable, onyx, nova, sage, shimmer or verse
- @{b}OpenAI Voice Instructions@{ub} - free text telling the model how to deliver the words, for example "speak slowly and calmly, like a documentary narrator". Supported by gpt-4o-mini-tts.

@{b}xAI@{ub}

- @{b}API key@{ub} and @{b}Voice@{ub} - ara, eve, leo, rex or sal
- @{b}Auto-insert speech tags into LLM replies@{ub} - asks the chat model to mark up its replies with the tags the xAI voice understands, so the delivery follows the sense of the text

@{b}ElevenLabs@{ub}

- @{b}API key@{ub}
- @{b}Model@{ub} - Eleven v3, Eleven Multilingual v2, Eleven Flash v2.5 or Eleven Turbo v2.5, with @{b}Fetch Models@{ub} to refresh the list
- @{b}Voice@{ub} - use @{b}Search@{ub} to look for a voice by name, or @{b}Get All Voices@{ub} to list everything on your account

@{b}OpenVox@{ub}

An OpenVox profile uses the connection settings above to reach your own OpenVox server, and @{b}Fetch Models@{ub} and the voice list are filled in from that server.

@{b}Missing components@{ub}

If a profile needs something that is not installed - narrator.device, translator.library, flite.device or AHI - AmigaGPT warns you which component is missing instead of failing silently.
@endnode

@node daemon "The AmigaGPTD daemon"
@{b}The AmigaGPTD daemon@{ub}

AmigaGPTD is a companion program with no user interface. It answers exactly the same ARexx commands as the full application, so scripts and other programs can use AmigaGPT without the GUI - and without MUI having to draw anything - which is much lighter on a classic Amiga.

@{b}Running it@{ub}

Run AmigaGPTD from the Shell, or let the installer add it to S:User-Startup so that it starts with the machine. It prints a short line when it starts, when it is ready and when it shuts down. Stop it with Ctrl-C or by closing its Shell.

@{b}Two different ARexx ports@{ub}

This is the point to watch when writing scripts:

  AmigaGPT   listens on   @{b}AMIGAGPT@{ub}
  AmigaGPTD  listens on   @{b}AMIGAGPTD@{ub}

The scripts supplied with AmigaGPT try AMIGAGPT first and fall back to AMIGAGPTD, which is a good pattern to copy:

  AMIGAGPT_PORT = "AMIGAGPT"
  IF ~SHOW('P',AMIGAGPT_PORT) THEN DO
    AMIGAGPT_PORT = "AMIGAGPTD"
    IF ~SHOW('P',AMIGAGPT_PORT) THEN DO
      SAY 'Cannot contact AmigaGPT. Please start either AmigaGPT or AmigaGPTD first.'
      EXIT 1
    END
  END
  ADDRESS VALUE AMIGAGPT_PORT

@{b}Conversation history@{ub}

The daemon keeps its own conversation in T:AmigaGPTD_history.json, so it remembers context from one ARexx call to the next. You can ask "What is the capital of France?" and then "What is its population?" and it will know you mean Paris. Because the history lives in T:, it lasts until the next reboot.

@{b}NEWCHAT@{ub} clears that history and starts a fresh conversation. See @{"NEWCHAT" link arexxnewchat}.

The daemon's history is entirely separate from the conversations shown in the GUI.

@{b}Configuration@{ub}

The daemon reads the same AMIGAGPT:config.json as the GUI, so the profiles, keys and models you set up in the application are the ones the daemon uses. It has no windows of its own, so use the GUI to change them.
@endnode

@node askgpt "The AskGPT shell command"
@{b}The AskGPT shell command@{ub}

AskGPT is an ARexx script that lets you ask questions from the Shell. The installer offers to put it in C: so it works from anywhere. It needs either AmigaGPT or AmigaGPTD to be running.

@{b}Usage@{ub}

  askgpt [GUI] [NEWCHAT] [PROFILE=name] question

- With no question at all, AskGPT asks you what you would like to know
- @{b}GUI@{ub} opens a small window for the question and the answer instead of printing to the Shell. This needs RxMUI and rmh.library; without them AskGPT falls back to a simple requester.
- @{b}NEWCHAT@{ub} clears the conversation history first, so the question is not answered in the context of whatever came before
- @{b}PROFILE=name@{ub}, or @{b}PR=name@{ub}, uses a particular chat profile instead of the active one

@{b}Examples@{ub}

  askgpt What is the capital of Australia?
  askgpt NEWCHAT Explain the Amiga copper in one paragraph
  askgpt PROFILE=Anthropic Claude Summarise this in three bullet points
  askgpt GUI

Because the daemon keeps its history between calls, successive askgpt commands continue the same conversation until you use NEWCHAT or reboot.
@endnode

@node arexx "ARexx Integration"
@{b}ARexx Integration@{ub}

AmigaGPT has a full ARexx port, so you can drive it from scripts and from any application that speaks ARexx. Send prompts, generate images and speak text with a handful of commands.

@{b}Which port to address@{ub}

  AmigaGPT   listens on   @{b}AMIGAGPT@{ub}
  AmigaGPTD  listens on   @{b}AMIGAGPTD@{ub}

Both understand the same commands, so a script written for one works with the other. See @{"The AmigaGPTD daemon" link daemon} for a snippet that connects to whichever is running.

@{b}MorphOS@{ub} does not yet have a complete ARexx implementation. Install the 68k version of ARexx on MorphOS to use ARexx with AmigaGPT.

@{b}Commands@{ub}

  @{"SENDMESSAGE       " link arexxsendmessage} send a prompt and get the reply
  @{"CREATEIMAGE       " link arexxcreateimage} generate an image
  @{"SPEAKTEXT         " link arexxspeaktext} speak text, or write it to an audio file
  @{"NEWCHAT           " link arexxnewchat} clear the daemon conversation history
  @{"The LIST commands " link arexxlists} discover profiles, models, voices, sizes, formats
  @{"Notes on arguments" link arexxargs} how the templates work

@{b}Examples and scripts@{ub}

  @{"Example script            " link arexxexample}
  @{"The scripts that are bundled" link arexxbundled}

@{b}The ARexx menu@{ub}

The @{b}ARexx@{ub} menu in the application has:

- @{b}ARexx Shell@{ub} - a toggle. Turning it on opens a Shell and puts all ARexx scripts into trace mode, so you can step through them line by line for debugging. Turning it off ends trace mode and closes the Shell.
- @{b}Import Script...@{ub} - adds a script to the ARexx menu so you can run it from there
- @{b}Run Script@{ub} - runs a script
@endnode

@node arexxargs "Notes on arguments"
@{b}Notes on arguments@{ub}

@{b}Reading the templates@{ub}

The command templates follow the usual AmigaDOS conventions:

  @{b}/K@{ub}  keyword. Must be given as NAME=value
  @{b}/N@{ub}  number
  @{b}/S@{ub}  switch. Present means on, absent means off
  @{b}/F@{ub}  the rest of the line. Must come last

Every argument has a short alias as well as its full name, so PROFILE and PR mean the same thing.

@{b}Use the full names for these five@{ub}

A few short aliases are shared by two arguments in the same template: @{b}S@{ub} is listed for both SYSTEM and SSL, @{b}PP@{ub} for both PROXYPORT and PROXYPASSWORD, and the help text prints @{b}P@{ub} for PORT where the command itself expects @{b}PO@{ub}. Spell these five out in full and there is no ambiguity:

  SYSTEM=...   SSL   PORT=...   PROXYPORT=...   PROXYPASSWORD=...

@{b}Quoting@{ub}

Prompts usually contain spaces. Because PROMPT is a /F argument it takes everything to the end of the line, so quoting is not required for the prompt itself, but keyword values that contain spaces do need quotes:

  'SENDMESSAGE PROFILE="My LM Studio" P=Tell me a joke about the Amiga'

@{b}Results@{ub}

Put OPTIONS RESULTS at the top of your script and read the answer from RESULT.

@{b}Overriding the configuration@{ub}

Anything you do not supply is taken from the profile that is currently active in AmigaGPT, so in the simplest case only the prompt is needed. Anything you do supply applies to that one command and is not saved.
@endnode

@node arexxsendmessage "SENDMESSAGE"
@{b}SENDMESSAGE@{ub}

Sends a message using a chat profile and returns the reply.

  SENDMESSAGE PR=PROFILE/K,M=MODEL/K,S=SYSTEM/K,SF=SYSTEMFILE/K,H=HOST/K,
              PO=PORT/N,S=SSL/S,K=APIKEY/K,U=USEPROXY/S,PH=PROXYHOST/K,
              PP=PROXYPORT/N,PS=PROXYUSESSSL/S,PA=PROXYREQUIRESAUTH/S,
              PU=PROXYUSERNAME/K,PP=PROXYPASSWORD/K,W=WEBSEARCH/S,P=PROMPT/F

@{b}Arguments@{ub}

- @{b}PROFILE@{ub} or @{b}PR@{ub} - the chat profile to use. A built-in name such as OpenAI, Google Gemini, xAI Grok or Anthropic Claude, or the name of a custom chat profile. Use @{b}LISTPROFILES@{ub} to see them. Defaults to the active chat profile.
- @{b}MODEL@{ub} or @{b}M@{ub} - the model to use. Use @{b}LISTMODELS@{ub} to see what a profile offers. Defaults to the profile's model.
- @{b}SYSTEM@{ub} - a system prompt for this request
- @{b}SYSTEMFILE@{ub} or @{b}SF@{ub} - a file whose contents are used as the system prompt. If both SYSTEMFILE and SYSTEM are given, the file contents come first and SYSTEM is appended to them.
- @{b}HOST@{ub} or @{b}H@{ub} - connect to this server instead of the profile's
- @{b}PORT@{ub} - the port to connect to
- @{b}SSL@{ub} - use an encrypted connection
- @{b}APIKEY@{ub} or @{b}K@{ub} - use this key instead of the profile's
- @{b}USEPROXY@{ub} or @{b}U@{ub} - route this request through a proxy
- @{b}PROXYHOST@{ub} or @{b}PH@{ub} - the proxy server address
- @{b}PROXYPORT@{ub} - the proxy port
- @{b}PROXYUSESSSL@{ub} or @{b}PS@{ub} - the proxy connection is encrypted
- @{b}PROXYREQUIRESAUTH@{ub} or @{b}PA@{ub} - the proxy needs a username and password
- @{b}PROXYUSERNAME@{ub} or @{b}PU@{ub} - the proxy username
- @{b}PROXYPASSWORD@{ub} - the proxy password
- @{b}WEBSEARCH@{ub} or @{b}W@{ub} - let the model search the web for this request. This can switch web search on for a profile that has it off; it cannot switch it off for a profile that has it on.
- @{b}PROMPT@{ub} or @{b}P@{ub} - required. The question to ask. Takes the rest of the line.

@{b}Result@{ub}

The reply text. It is cleaned up before it is returned - no escape backslashes and no escaped markdown characters - so it can be printed or fed straight into another program.

@{b}Notes@{ub}

The Shell Tool works through SENDMESSAGE as well. If the profile has it enabled, you will be asked to approve each command the model wants to run.

When the daemon answers, the message is added to its conversation history, so the next SENDMESSAGE continues the same conversation. See @{"The AmigaGPTD daemon" link daemon}.

@{b}Examples@{ub}

  'SENDMESSAGE P=What is the capital of France?'
  SAY RESULT

  'SENDMESSAGE PROFILE=Anthropic Claude M=claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929 P=Write a haiku about the Amiga'
  SAY RESULT

  'SENDMESSAGE SYSTEM=Answer in French only P=What is an Amiga?'
  SAY RESULT

  'SENDMESSAGE WEBSEARCH P=What is the latest AmigaOS release?'
  SAY RESULT
@endnode

@node arexxcreateimage "CREATEIMAGE"
@{b}CREATEIMAGE@{ub}

Generates an image and returns the path of the file it was saved to.

  CREATEIMAGE PR=PROFILE/K,M=MODEL/K,S=SIZE/K,K=APIKEY/K,D=DESTINATION/K,P=PROMPT/F

@{b}Arguments@{ub}

- @{b}PROFILE@{ub} or @{b}PR@{ub} - the image profile to use. A built-in name such as OpenAI, Google Gemini or xAI Grok, or a custom image profile. Use @{b}LISTPROFILES@{ub}. Defaults to the active image profile.
- @{b}MODEL@{ub} or @{b}M@{ub} - the image model. Use @{b}LISTMODELS@{ub}. Defaults to the profile's model.
- @{b}SIZE@{ub} or @{b}S@{ub} - the image size. Use @{b}LISTIMAGESIZES@{ub}. Defaults to 1024x1024. Some providers ignore this.
- @{b}APIKEY@{ub} or @{b}K@{ub} - use this key instead of the profile's
- @{b}DESTINATION@{ub} or @{b}D@{ub} - where to write the picture. If you leave this out, a temporary file is created.
- @{b}PROMPT@{ub} or @{b}P@{ub} - required. A description of the picture. Takes the rest of the line.

@{b}Result@{ub}

The full path of the file that was written, which is exactly what you need to hand to MultiView or a datatype.

@{b}Example@{ub}

  'CREATEIMAGE D=Work:Pictures/amiga.png P=A beautiful Amiga computer on a desk'
  SAY 'Image saved to:' RESULT
  ADDRESS COMMAND
  'MultiView Work:Pictures/amiga.png'
@endnode

@node arexxspeaktext "SPEAKTEXT"
@{b}SPEAKTEXT@{ub}

Speaks the given text aloud, or writes it to an audio file.

  SPEAKTEXT PR=PROFILE/K,M=MODEL/K,V=VOICE/K,I=INSTRUCTIONS/K,K=APIKEY/K,
            O=OUTPUT/K,F=FORMAT/K,P=PROMPT/F

@{b}Arguments@{ub}

- @{b}PROFILE@{ub} or @{b}PR@{ub} - the speech profile to use. A built-in speech system name or a custom speech profile. Use @{b}LISTPROFILES@{ub}. Defaults to the active speech profile.
- @{b}MODEL@{ub} or @{b}M@{ub} - the voice model, for profiles that have one. Use @{b}LISTMODELS@{ub}.
- @{b}VOICE@{ub} or @{b}V@{ub} - the voice. Use @{b}LISTVOICES@{ub}. For a Workbench narrator profile, the accepted values are @{b}male@{ub}, @{b}female@{ub}, @{b}male robot@{ub} and @{b}female robot@{ub}, which override the profile's sex and mode.
- @{b}INSTRUCTIONS@{ub} or @{b}I@{ub} - delivery instructions for the OpenAI voices
- @{b}APIKEY@{ub} or @{b}K@{ub} - use this key instead of the profile's
- @{b}OUTPUT@{ub} or @{b}O@{ub} - write the audio to this file instead of playing it
- @{b}FORMAT@{ub} or @{b}F@{ub} - the audio format for OUTPUT. Use @{b}LISTAUDIOFORMATS@{ub}. The default is mp3. Ignored when OUTPUT is not given.
- @{b}PROMPT@{ub} or @{b}P@{ub} - required. The text to speak. Takes the rest of the line.

@{b}Examples@{ub}

  'SPEAKTEXT P=Hello from ARexx!'

  'SPEAKTEXT V=nova P=Good morning'

  'SPEAKTEXT O=RAM:greeting.wav F=wav P=Welcome to AmigaGPT'
@endnode

@node arexxnewchat "NEWCHAT"
@{b}NEWCHAT@{ub}

Clears the daemon's conversation history and starts a fresh conversation. Use it when you want a question answered on its own, without the context of everything that came before.

  NEWCHAT

The command takes no arguments and returns "Conversation history cleared" when it succeeds.

See @{"The AmigaGPTD daemon" link daemon}.
@endnode

@node arexxlists "The LIST commands"
@{b}The LIST commands@{ub}

These commands let a script discover what is available instead of hard-coding it.

@{b}LISTPROFILES@{ub}

Lists every chat, image and speech profile.

  LISTPROFILES

@{b}LISTMODELS@{ub}

Fetches the models a profile's server currently offers, so the answer reflects the server rather than only AmigaGPT's built-in lists.

  LISTMODELS PR=PROFILE/K

- @{b}PROFILE@{ub} or @{b}PR@{ub} - the profile to inspect. The same name can exist as a chat, an image and a speech profile, and LISTMODELS returns a section for each match. The aliases ChatGPT, Gemini, Grok and Claude are also accepted. With no PROFILE it returns a section for each of the three active profiles.

@{b}LISTVOICES@{ub}

Lists the voices available for a speech profile.

  LISTVOICES PR=PROFILE/K

- @{b}PROFILE@{ub} or @{b}PR@{ub} - the speech profile to inspect. Defaults to the active one. For an ElevenLabs profile the voices are fetched from your ElevenLabs account. For a Workbench narrator profile it returns male, female, male robot and female robot.

@{b}LISTIMAGESIZES@{ub}

Lists every image size AmigaGPT knows about.

  LISTIMAGESIZES

@{b}LISTAUDIOFORMATS@{ub}

Lists the audio formats SPEAKTEXT can write: pcm, mp3, opus, wav, aac and flac.

  LISTAUDIOFORMATS

@{b}?@{ub}

Prints the whole command set with its templates.

  ?
@endnode

@node arexxexample "Example script"
@{b}Example script@{ub}

  /* Simple ARexx script for AmigaGPT */
  OPTIONS RESULTS

  /* Talk to whichever of the two is running */
  AMIGAGPT_PORT = "AMIGAGPT"
  IF ~SHOW('P',AMIGAGPT_PORT) THEN DO
    AMIGAGPT_PORT = "AMIGAGPTD"
    IF ~SHOW('P',AMIGAGPT_PORT) THEN DO
      SAY 'Cannot contact AmigaGPT. Please start either AmigaGPT or AmigaGPTD first.'
      EXIT 1
    END
  END
  ADDRESS VALUE AMIGAGPT_PORT

  /* Start a fresh conversation */
  'NEWCHAT'

  /* Ask a question */
  'SENDMESSAGE P=What is the capital of France?'
  SAY RESULT

  /* Follow it up - the daemon remembers the context */
  'SENDMESSAGE P=What is its population?'
  SAY RESULT

  /* Generate an image */
  'CREATEIMAGE P=A beautiful Amiga computer on a desk'
  SAY 'Image saved to:' RESULT

  /* Speak some text */
  'SPEAKTEXT P=Hello from ARexx!'

  EXIT 0
@endnode

@node arexxbundled "The scripts that are bundled"
@{b}The scripts that are bundled@{ub}

AmigaGPT installs a set of example scripts into AMIGAGPT:rexx. They are worth reading as well as running, because between them they demonstrate most of what the ARexx port can do.

- @{b}askgpt.rexx@{ub} - the Shell front end. Argument parsing, an optional RxMUI interface, profile selection and NEWCHAT. See @{"The AskGPT shell command" link askgpt}.
- @{b}help.rexx@{ub} - prints the command set, the profiles, the models for the active profiles and the voices for the active speech profile. A good first thing to run.
- @{b}joke.rexx@{ub} - asks for a joke and shows it in an RxMUI window, falling back to a plain requester when RxMUI is not installed
- @{b}say.rexx@{ub} - asks you what to say and speaks it
- @{b}say_os_version.rexx@{ub} - runs the Version command and speaks the result. Shows how to feed the output of an AmigaDOS command into AmigaGPT.
- @{b}amiga_image.rexx@{ub} - generates a picture and opens it in MultiView
- @{b}textedit_translate_norwegian.rexx@{ub} - takes the selected text in TextEdit, translates it and puts the translation back. Shows AmigaGPT working as a service to another application.

Use @{b}Import Script...@{ub} in the @{b}ARexx@{ub} menu to add your own scripts to the menu.
@endnode

@node menus "Menu Reference"
@{b}Menu Reference@{ub}

@{b}Project@{ub}

  @{b}Print@{ub}                Right-Amiga + P   @{"print the conversation" link printing}
  @{b}About AmigaGPT...@{ub}                      version and credits
  @{b}About MUI...@{ub}                           the MUI version in use
